"""Editorial content. PR decision text is captured separately, without invented reasons."""

EPISODES = [
 dict(id='host', n='01', title='Give command syntax to the host.', short='The handoff', prs=[129,132,135,136],
      takeaway='CLI chooses syntax, workspace and environment. Daemon owns execution machinery; an injected host module still supplies the command program. Renderer formats lifecycle reports.',
      before=['CLI: argv + daemon machinery','Worker: imports CLI execution','Telemetry: resolves state path'],
      after=['CLI: commandName + opaque argv','Daemon worker: loads executor URL','Renderer: lifecycle bytes'],
      question='In “refs def”, is the operational command refs or def?', answer='refs. The target stays in argv; the command label comes from the CLI’s syntax classification.',
      links=[('policy','Who may accept that command?'),('state','What does the executor keep?'),('ownership','When does the CLI use the package?')],
      extras=[
       ('H1','The protocol moves from generation 4 to 5. commandName participates in duplicate compatibility; a mixed-generation peer takes compatibility handling before execution.','stated','PR #132 ties the version change to authoritative command metadata.','protocol'),
       ('H2','The host executor returns ordered stdout/stderr bytes and an exit code. The worker validates module/results, rechunks, sequences and acknowledges durable consumption; diagnostics remain JSON-like and resource sampling has no host payload.','stated','PR #135 assigns protocol mechanics to the worker while keeping the daemon independent of CLI/core.','executor'),
       ('H3','CLI opens a workspace before command validation, prepares after validation, and falls back to local command handling when workspace resolution fails. State paths resolve once, including symlinked existing ancestors and missing tails.','stated','PRs #128–129 preserve observable error/read order and the same canonical state string; the architecture plan preserves local routing errors.','coordinator'),
       ('H4','Lifecycle text/JSON bytes move to renderer; CLI still selects format, writes bytes and wraps existing errors. Telemetry remains a leaf receiving the resolved path.','stated','PRs #129 and #136 specify these ownership boundaries and output preservation.','render'),
       ('H5','One frozen command tuple supplies runtime validation and the union; an exhaustive CLI record separates workspace commands from help/version/control/unknown routes. Environment/home values are injected for isolated path tests; lifecycle formatting uses one stateless class.','stated','PRs #129, #132 and #136 explain deterministic tests, syntax ownership and the cohesive rendering unit.','selector'),
       ('H6','The state-directory layer also changes tests: a worker fixture waits for three files; startup-owner assertions tolerate transient missing reads and poll full owner identity, with 5-s ownership and 15-s readiness waits. That status scenario is later removed from the CLI suite.','unexplained','The #129 commits name synchronization/oracle stabilization. No separate reason was found for including these test changes in path ownership or for the exact waits. #149’s retirement is shown in episode 09.','status-test-129'),
      ], sources=['selector','coordinator','executor','render','state-directory','protocol']),

 dict(id='state', n='02', title='Publish a view. Keep its toolchain behind it.', short='Workspace publication', prs=[123,124,126],
      takeaway='Core takes source caching, revision/index publication and project membership. TypeScript keeps parsing, projects, source handles and reversible toolchain mutations. These are separate publication boundaries.',
      before=['TypeScript: source bytes','TypeScript: revision index','TypeScript: graph + toolchain'],
      after=['Core: replace byte snapshot','Core: prepare → validate → publish','TypeScript: prepare / commit / rollback'],
      question='Select a.ts alone. Is b.ts gone everywhere?', answer='No. Its source bytes are evicted, but a selection refresh retains its prepared index entry. “The cache” is more than one thing.',
      links=[('turn','When do query answers expire?'),('host','Who owns the language adapter?')],
      extras=[
       ('S1','A FileSystem facade caches source bytes by absolute path + change token and replaces snapshot coverage, even for selection. Unchanged tracked files reuse bytes; changed/omitted files lose bytes; untracked reads pass through without retention.','stated','PR #123 preserves replacement and selection eviction, defers selection-aware retention, and keeps filesystem injection usable by existing services.','source-cache'),
       ('S2','Prepared state treats workspace refresh as replacement and selection as an overlay. Validation precedes toolchain commit, then one index assignment; failures invoke rollback. ensureFiles keeps earlier one-file progress if a later file fails.','stated','PR #124 explains incremental preparation, pre-commit validation and sequential progress.','revision'),
       ('S3','Core validates canonical membership and every active input; a fresh rebuild observes missing inputs and drops unreachable observations. FIFO config discovery keeps all project owners in order; the LAST configured owner is primary, with inferred fallback.','unexplained','Validation/collector reasons are stated in #126. No separate reason for last-owner precedence was found; commits explicitly characterize and publish the order.','project-graph'),
       ('S4','A failed portable candidate leaves its prior index/graph published. This is not a transaction across source bytes, graph and all backends: TypeScript refresh touches the source snapshot before graph and index preparation.','unexplained','The per-owner transaction is stated. The plan does not separately reconcile whole-refresh failure with these already-progressed owners.','backend'),
       ('S5','TypeScript journals source updates/removals and stages paths reversibly to preserve exact SourceFile and node handles. Core retains unchanged prepared/declaration identities rather than rebuilding every object.','stated','PR #124 explicitly preserves source/node identity and keeps ts-morph mutation ownership in TypeScript.','typescript-state'),
      ], sources=['source-cache','revision','project-graph','typescript-state','backend','source-cache-main']),

 dict(id='turn', n='03', title='Keep the workspace. Empty the query answers.', short='The next turn', prs=[127,128],
      takeaway='One retained core session can serve another request. Six query stores empty together after successful refresh or at release. TypeScript owns their keys and algorithms; backend release now awaits project cleanup.',
      before=['CLI: request-scope lifetime','TypeScript: six Maps + clear list','Backend: project promise can detach'],
      after=['Core: retained WorkspaceSession','Service owns one core scope + six handles','Backend: awaited release boundary'],
      question='Project cleanup is still pending. Can backend release already be fulfilled?', answer='With the controlled asynchronous collaborator: main says yes; the tip waits. Emptying a cache and completing cleanup are different events.',
      links=[('state','What survived the refresh?'),('queue','Who asks a retained worker to release?'),('delivery','Which other completion can still be pending?')],
      extras=[
       ('T1','The service owns one scope instance and six separate stores: definitions, references, targets, callers, callees and positions. Five store promises; positions store locations. Keys and query algorithms stay in TypeScript.','stated','PR #127 preserves independent key/value spaces, identities and algorithms.','queries'),
       ('T2','Cache undefined by presence; store the exact returned value/promise. A synchronous factory throw is not inserted; a rejected promise stays cached. Reset empties stores without cancelling issued promises or destroying handles. Reference projections and position-node lookups still happen outside stored results.','unexplained','Map.has and preserving existing identities/failure behavior are stated. No separate rationale was located for the minimal exported handle API or each preserved projection choice.','scope'),
       ('T3','Only successful refresh begins a new semantic turn, even if revisions are unchanged. Failed refresh retains semantic answers; it does not roll back every earlier owner. Release clears synchronously, then awaits projects; rejection propagates through backend release.','stated','PR #127 explains clear-first and success-only invalidation and discloses an awaited backend barrier.','backend'),
       ('T4','The async release barrier changes completion relative to main when project release is asynchronous. The PR discloses the await; why that contract is compatible with the architecture plan’s unchanged timing/failure promise is UNEXPLAINED.','unexplained','Inspected: supplied PR body/commit messages, exact main/tip sources and architecture/follow-up specs. Concrete TypeScript project cleanup is synchronous; the pending probe injects an async project.','backend-main'),
       ('T5','Session retention reuses discovery; cold request retention does not. Selection discovers freshly to avoid siblings. Backends are copied/frozen; each turn gets a fresh router. Open precedes validation, preparation follows it. Concurrent Promise.all release leaves the session reusable; later calls retry every backend.','stated','PR #128 explains discovery isolation, router lifetime, observable error/read ordering and repeatable release.','session'),
       ('T6','The #127 delta adds six service and four scope cases without editing the five old service test bodies. Delayed release tests pin pending/rejection behavior; delayed-success and daemon scheduling are not new witnesses in that six-file diff.','unexplained','Tests characterize the chosen boundary; no separate explanation for those particular omissions was found. This page records a controlled delayed-success main/tip probe.','queries-test'),
      ], sources=['session','scope','queries','backend','backend-main','queries-test','scope-test']),

 dict(id='policy', n='04', title='Carry one policy. Stop at the first guard.', short='Policy & admission', prs=[130,131,133,134],
      takeaway='A frozen, complete daemon policy crosses process and worker boundaries. Required slices replace scattered defaults. Closed failure/rejection vocabularies own precedence and retry meaning; the first failed admission guard wins.',
      before=['CLI-local thresholds / partial options','Repeated failure labels','Inline admission + retry booleans'],
      after=['DaemonPolicy → process → worker','Outer failures ≠ worker failures','auth → ready → resource → queue → identity'],
      question='A caller is unauthenticated, the worker is unready, and resources are pressured. Which condition speaks?', answer='Authentication disconnects first. Later reasons do not run. With authentication fixed, not-ready wins before resource-pressure.',
      links=[('wire','How are guards expressed on a socket?'),('recovery','What do the two retry counters count?'),('queue','Who acts on resource policy?')],
      extras=[
       ('P1','Seven policy families cover transport, startup, shutdown, delivery, output, resources and diagnostics. Values are derived once, frozen and exactly validated across hops; no user flags/environment/config tune them. Serialization/override factories end package-internal; AST, declaration, runtime, manifest and dependency checks enforce that surface.','stated','Architecture and policy plans require one complete snapshot and no user tuning. #130 stages snapshot transport and enforceable ownership; #131 adopts it. Temporary public bridges retire later.','policy-plan'),
       ('P2','Status gets 100 ms, ordinary lifecycle 250 ms, admission 5 s. Accepted completion, healthy warm-up, startup silence, worker-output acknowledgement and unacknowledged result retention have no corresponding deadline/eviction here. Silence, retention and idle-accounting changes are deferred.','stated','The policy record names these distinct purposes and intentional absences; follow-up specs retain the deferred work.','policy-plan'),
       ('P3','Resource limits derive from effective constrained/total memory with clamps and hysteresis. Output has chunk/inline/per-result/aggregate bounds; diagnostics and replacements have independent retention/capacity limits. The deeper numeric table is the same seven-family policy.','stated','The policy record supplies a reason per threshold/recipe; these reasons explain purpose and preservation, not empirical proof that each exact number is optimal.','policy'),
       ('P4','Worker wrapper deserializes the complete policy before new Worker. A malformed injected snapshot can now throw in the parent constructor before a worker or its startup handlers exist.','unexplained','This source-order consequence accompanies #131 chunk-limit adoption. No separate reason for moving this malformed-input failure boundary was found.','worker'),
       ('P5','Admission order is authentication, readiness, resource pause, queue, then duplicate compatibility. Compatibility reads do not mutate the ledger. Authentication disconnects; not-ready/draining/resource-pressure are retry-safe, incompatible is not. Contradictory code/boolean frames are corrupt. The staging request field stays unknown to avoid a CLI type dependency.','stated','PR #134 centralizes first-failure order and code-derived retry safety while preserving dependency direction.','admission'),
       ('P6','Outer failure precedence is resource interruption → result capacity → worker exit (or stopping) → shutdown code → stopping/internal. Worker failures use a separate four-word vocabulary; concrete instanceof facts stay with mechanism owners.','stated','PR #133 separates domains and makes classification pure while preserving subtype behavior.','failures'),
       ('P7','Test adapters can change the stimulus: inline zero may become a positive floor (transport: at least one 64-KiB chunk); a legacy memoryCapBytes option is accepted but not forwarded. Empty-directory assertions may therefore observe an empty directory without a spill.','unexplained','Test-only tuning is stated in #131. The exact compatibility floors and ignored option are not separately justified in the supplied record.','transport-helper'),
       ('P8','Spool fixtures now coordinate chunk/inline/result limits and enlarge payloads; a capture threshold changes 1→32. The benchmark resource-policy object gains replacement window/limit fields while schemaVersion remains 1. These are test/reporting decisions, alongside production policy.','unexplained','No separate rationale for every changed fixture threshold or unversioned benchmark field was located.','benchmark'),
       ('P9','Accepted execute reattachment and result-fetch resume use independent numeric counters. Factory defaults are one reattachment and one fetch per execute attempt; a reattached attempt gets a fresh inner allowance. Neither counter authorizes local replay of accepted work.','stated','PR #131 explains the two scopes; #142 gives their final execution-client owner. Episode 06 works through the retained identity and fresh capture.','execution-client'),
      ], sources=['policy','policy-plan','admission','failures','worker','transport-helper','process-helper','benchmark']),

 dict(id='wire', n='05', title='Give each socket job one owner.', short='Bytes & connections', prs=[137,139,140,141,143],
      takeaway='Framing, validation, bounded exchanges, outbound mechanics and inbound serving become separate owners. Decoder lifetime is one connection; raw socket closure alone cannot decide request retry safety.',
      before=['LocalDaemonTransport: all framing','Same facade: sockets + exchanges','Same facade: bind / serve / close'],
      after=['WireCodec + ProtocolValidator','SocketClient → Lifecycle / Execution clients','SocketServer: per-connection chains'],
      question='Two bytes of a four-byte frame prefix arrive. Is that a request?', answer='No. The decoder buffers until a whole frame exists. Decoding still does not establish semantic validity, correlation or acceptance.',
      links=[('recovery','What survives a fresh decoder?'),('policy','Who chooses the timeout?'),('ownership','What replaces the temporary facade?')],
      extras=[
       ('W1','Control decoders accept JSON; transfer decoders additionally accept flagged binary chunks. Ordinary lifecycle JSON keeps its larger cap; execution control uses the smaller cap. Chunk headers/digests belong to the wire codec; semantic/correlation checks belong to validation.','stated','PR #137 distinguishes connection purpose, frame capacity and integrity ownership.','codec'),
       ('W2','Socket reads are pull-driven and pause after each chunk. Outbound writes keep call-order FIFO under backpressure. Inbound connections each own response/write chains; one connection does not serialize every client.','stated','PRs #139 and #141 explain demand, ordered writing and connection isolation.','socket-client'),
       ('W3','Lifecycle/status/ack exchanges share correlation, bounded response and cleanup; acknowledgement stays off the general lifecycle requester port. Validation occurs before accepted delivery state. Composition purpose chooses timeouts even for the same ping.','stated','PR #140 keeps capability and delivery-state distinctions visible.','lifecycle'),
       ('W4','Server endpoint probes reuse the socket client. One shutdown promise is shared while force can escalate an ongoing graceful drain. Raw EOF/errors stay separate from authenticated protocol outcomes.','stated','PRs #139–141 preserve the protocol/mechanism split and concurrent shutdown settlement.','socket-server'),
       ('W5','#143 makes LocalDaemonTransport a required-policy delegate and removes canFrame; injected-component identity tests and a source-mechanism ban protect that boundary. #149 deletes the facade and composes the split owners directly in DaemonTransportFactory. Shared transport-error classification survives the split.','stated','PRs #140, #143 and #149 explain common error meaning, direct injection, architectural checks and final composition.','transport'),
      ], sources=['codec','validator','socket-client','socket-server','lifecycle','transport']),

 dict(id='recovery', n='06', title='Resume bytes; reattach identity.', short='Interrupted results', prs=[138,142],
      takeaway='Result-fetch keeps one capture and durable record offset. Execute reattachment keeps request identity but starts a new capture and fetch budget. Accepted failure is not a signal to rerun locally.',
      before=['Facade: capture + manifest + resume','Facade: accepted retry orchestration','CLI output store reused for warm output'],
      after=['Receiver: durable offset + manifest','ExecutionClient: two recovery budgets','Daemon: capture + disposal ownership'],
      question='Record 0 arrived, but append is still pending. Is nextOffset 0 or 1?', answer='0. Only fulfilled append advances the cursor to 1. A new connection reuses that durable cursor and the same manifest.',
      links=[('queue','Why does the same ID not execute twice?'),('delivery','Where do fetched bytes come from?'),('wire','Why is the decoder new?')],
      extras=[
       ('R1','One receiver retains manifest identity, digest progress and the next RECORD offset across fetch connections; each connection gets a fresh decoder. Offset advances after awaited append, never on receipt alone.','stated','PR #138 gives durable capture and wire framing different lifetimes.','receiver'),
       ('R2','A reattached execute attempt allocates fresh output and a fresh per-attempt fetch allowance. Both counters are numeric and independent (factory defaults: one fetch and one reattachment). Only eligible authenticated accepted closure can trigger reattachment.','stated','PRs #131 and #142 explicitly separate the two recovery scopes.','execution-client'),
       ('R3','At the tip, repeated eligible fetch closures can consume the configured allowance; exhausted fetch-ended becomes accepted corruption. Accepted-close reattachment exhaustion preserves the original accepted-close error. This supersedes the intermediate #131 later-completion-error behavior.','stated','PR #142 says exhaustion retains accepted-corruption behavior; its implementation stores originalAcceptedClose. This page describes the final delta, not #131 as the final transport.','execution-client'),
       ('R4','Manifest/end coordinates, record count, byte count and digest must agree. Failed capture is disposed; successful finish transfers disposal to the caller for replay/ack cleanup. ACK uses a narrow completion-only lifecycle-client capability. Warm capture/storage and controlled failure outputs belong to daemon.','stated','PRs #138, #142 and #148 specify capture ownership, transfer cleanup and the narrow ACK capability.','capture'),
       ('R5','Before accepted completion, only retry-safe transport failure can fall back locally. Once receipt is accepted, completion failure returns controlled warm failure output, not a second local execution.','stated','The architecture and #148 keep route, output and replay-safety decisions inside the client.','client-runtime'),
      ], sources=['receiver','execution-client','capture','client-runtime','transport-error']),

 dict(id='queue', n='07', title='Accept once. Run one turn at a time.', short='Execution & generations', prs=[144,145,147],
      takeaway='The ledger owns immutable acceptance identity. An execution session coordinates the FIFO queue, worker, delivery and resource sampling. A generation manager fences replaced workers; activity is projected from snapshots.',
      before=['WorkspaceDaemon: turn coordination','Process: worker fields + replacement','Process: formats live activity'],
      after=['ExecutionSession → ledger / FIFO','GenerationManager → worker generations','ActivityProjector ← explicit snapshots'],
      question='Q42 returns after its socket breaks. Does the daemon accept a second turn?', answer='A matching duplicate returns its original acceptance. No new FIFO turn, clock read, trace or idle reset is created.',
      links=[('recovery','What is the reconnecting caller doing?'),('turn','Which caches belong to the retained worker?'),('delivery','When is the next turn allowed?')],
      extras=[
       ('Q1','Ledger entries keep original acceptedAt and queuePosition through queued/running/terminal/acknowledged states. Compatibility includes commandName and canonical request fields; a duplicate never replays acceptance side effects. The ledger remains daemon-lifetime state, with eviction deferred.','stated','PR #147 explains one immutable identity and daemon-lifetime idempotence; the follow-up spec defers ledger eviction.','ledger'),
       ('Q2','FIFO turn work includes spool finish, ledger completion and ONE read of the currently tracked delivery promise; a resource sample scheduled in finally gates the next turn after success or failure. Normal turn completion does not await every future fetch or ordinary ACK.','stated','PRs #146–147 specify the latest-stream barrier and turn-boundary sampling. The single lookup is visible in source.','accepted-session'),
       ('Q3','One generation manager handles startup, execution correlation, replacement, fencing, release and shutdown. Initial protocol readiness is activated after warm sampling; replacement callers share one operation. Graceful close and forced terminate have separate cached promises.','stated','PR #145 separates generation lifecycle from resource recovery/circuit policy and supports forced interruption of graceful close.','generations'),
       ('Q4','A replacement starts the next worker before awaiting old-worker termination; readiness is withheld and old-generation exit reports are fenced. Resource interruption classification remains separate from worker-exit classification.','stated','#145 commits characterize replacement order; its manager and recovery port preserve the explicit handoff.','generations'),
       ('Q5','Activity is a pure projection of explicit process/clock, queue, worker and last sampled resource values. Replacement generation comes from worker; spoolBytes comes from the last resource sample. Legacy pong can retain fileCount while activity hides it before readiness.','stated','PR #144 gives specific reasons for each of these data sources and legacy differences.','activity'),
       ('Q6','Wall and monotonic time stay distinct. The standalone queue default changes from Date.now to daemon monotonic time; the old shipped process already injected monotonic time. Idle reset remains acceptance-based, not readiness- or completion-based.','unexplained','Clock ownership and deferring idle changes are stated in #148. No separate reason for the standalone default’s changed clock domain was found.','queue'),
       ('Q7','Existing SHA-256 canonical request hashing now includes a commandName/request envelope; object sorting, undefined omission and array order are retained. One registry matcher checks identity+instance plus supplied token/owner/revision/time, strengthening some old instance-only comparisons.','unexplained','Command identity is explained in #132; registry centralization is named in #148 commits. No separate reason for each strengthened registry expectation was found. The hashing algorithm itself is not new in this stack.','registry'),
      ], sources=['ledger','accepted-session','queue','generations','activity','registry','lifetime']),

 dict(id='delivery', n='08', title='Finish the work. Then finish delivery.', short='Completion & ACK', prs=[146],
      takeaway='A delivery session coordinates attachments, retained output, acknowledgement and traces. Ledger state and spool bytes keep their owners. Stream completion, logical ACK and physical cleanup are distinct boundaries.',
      before=['Process: stream maps + trace handles','Ledger: accepted state','Spool store: output bytes'],
      after=['DeliverySession: coordination only','Ledger: same state authority','Spool: cleanup before logical ACK'],
      question='Deleting the completion spool fails. Does a valid ACK still receive success?', answer='Yes. Cleanup failure is diagnostic; the journal still acknowledges and the protocol returns result-acknowledged.',
      links=[('queue','Which delivery promise gates a turn?'),('recovery','How does the receiver hand off disposal?'),('ownership','What can an outside test inspect?')],
      extras=[
       ('D1','Attachments get the original acceptance and subscribe to terminal state. The newest tracked attached-delivery promise replaces the previous map entry; settling an older promise cannot remove a newer one. Execution captures the current promise once.','stated','PR #146 prefers one latest per-request promise over accumulating every duplicate attachment.','delivery'),
       ('D2','Normal FIFO progress waits for that captured attached stream, not an ordinary ACK. Workspace-deletion handling and shutdown have explicit bounded ACK waits; later fetches do not retroactively extend an already-captured turn barrier.','stated','The narrow execution/delivery/shutdown coordination is explicit in #146–147 and the final process/session code.','accepted-session'),
       ('D3','ACK authenticates the transfer, attempts physical cleanup, records cleanup failure, then logically acknowledges and replies successfully. A missing/corrupt stored completion can instead invalidate its ledger completion and emit internal failure.','stated','PR #146 explicitly keeps cleanup failure diagnostic rather than withholding protocol success; its delivery owner retains spool-read failure handling.','delivery'),
       ('D4','Disconnected traces have time/count retention and fenced handles: late events stop once evicted. Trace expiry is not result eviction. Completion storage and accepted identity can outlive a socket and the trace that described it.','stated','PR #146 separates trace retention from authoritative ledger/spool ownership; the policy plan defers result eviction.','delivery'),
      ], sources=['delivery','accepted-session','process','spool']),

 dict(id='ownership', n='09', title='Make the new owner the only owner.', short='The final cut', prs=[148,149],
      takeaway='#148 stages package mechanisms while CLI still runs a frozen compatibility graph. #149 switches CLI to DaemonClient and removes app copies. Tests move to package internals or a read-only inspector; some observations narrow.',
      before=['main: CLI owns daemon mechanisms','#148: package added; CLI graph still active','External tests read app-owned storage'],
      after=['#149: CLI → DaemonClient','Daemon owns process / workers / transport','External tests → read-only /testing'],
      question='At #148, does moving the worker into a package change the running CLI path?', answer='No. The active CLI switch is #149. Package relocation and production cutover are two separate decisions.',
      links=[('host','What does the host still supply?'),('policy','Which bridges were temporary?'),('queue','What survives inside the new owner?')],
      extras=[
       ('O1','The final daemon package is an internal-dependency leaf. CLI classifies/composes; TypeScript depends on core; renderer on core+daemon; telemetry stays a leaf. LocalDaemonTransport is gone and its split owners compose package-locally. No new process hop is created.','stated','The architecture plan locks import direction and retains process/thread topology. #149 replaces the compatibility facade with focused package composition.','rules'),
       ('O2','Public paths are exactly root, /process-entry, /worker-entry, /testing. Root declarations are Node-free; DaemonClient starts one dynamic runtime import at construction and retains its promise, including rejection. Optional host policy defaults to currentSystem.','unexplained','Node-free facade is stated in #148. No separate reason was found for constructor-start loading, sticky failure or the optional host-policy fallback.','client'),
       ('O3','Lazy routing reads/observes once and stops at the first route: disabled goes local without registry observation; absent starts independently; starting/recovering stay cold; dead/incompatible fall back and trigger; responsive ready goes warm.','stated','PR #148 explains ordered lazy guards and package-owned routing, so the first choice prevents later observation side effects.','client-runtime'),
       ('O4','Authentication remains operation-specific: identify/terminate/kill require instance+token before the ordinary protocol check; ping/stop use protocol+instance; execute/status/fetch/ack also require token. Relocation preserves this partition.','unexplained','No separate reason for the retained operation-specific partition was found; it is not a newly introduced bypass.','process'),
       ('O5','The temporary policy-testing export retires after mechanism tests move package-local; serialization methods retire once entries move. Compiler-backed imports/exports/clock/storage inventories replace compatibility aliases and constrain syntax that could cross the boundary.','stated','The #148 policy plan supplies explicit retirement conditions; #149 supplies the enforcement rationale.','policy-plan-148'),
       ('O6','The inspector exposes parsed diagnostics, instance summaries and spool counts/bytes, with deterministic order. Missing directories yield empty/false/zero; other I/O errors propagate. It skips malformed diagnostic lines, rejects invalid cursors and reads older backups before the active file.','unexplained','Read-only observability is stated in #149. No separate reason was located for each filtering/error/order choice.','inspector'),
       ('O7','CLI diagnostic assertions switch from raw log bytes/rotation to parsed events; skipped malformed lines make parsed absence weaker than raw-byte absence. Seven status and three stop scenarios disappear from those CLI suites; package tests cover some related behavior, not identical witnesses.','unexplained','Package observability and moving tests are stated. No per-assertion rationale establishes equivalence for these removed CLI observations.','diagnostic-test'),
       ('O8','The mocked daemon-entry handoff test (including 257-MiB worker limit) is removed. A worker readiness case loses three startup/four result-duration expectations and its CLI-version mismatch case; generic executor and built-entry tests replace the app-specific seam.','unexplained','Package-independent actors/executor injection is stated; loss of those exact assertions is not separately justified.','entry-test-main'),
       ('O9','A serial package Vitest suite, tsx fixture dependency, platform cleanup, and removal of temporary export/lint assertions accompany migration. The temporary export has a retirement reason; that does not supply reasons for every scheduling or assertion change.','unexplained','Inspected package/test configuration, #148–149 commits and plans. None gives separate rationale for every listed choice.','vitest'),
       ('O10','#148 freezes 38 app-local production copies with a source-content digest, normalized across checkout line endings; #149 replaces the temporary freeze with deletion/reachability enforcement. A changed digest must be updated as a unit.','unexplained','Staging and line-ending portability are stated in #148 and its commits. No separate reason for the exact aggregate digest strategy was found. This is a source-copy guard, distinct from accepted-request hashing.','compatibility-148'),
       ('O11','Warm result capture, disposal and controlled failure outputs are package-owned, rather than supplied by the host. Idle timing still resets at acceptance; changing it to readiness/completion is deferred. These lifetimes travel with the mechanism move.','stated','PR #148 explicitly assigns capture/replay cleanup to the client and preserves acceptance-based idle accounting during extraction. Episodes 06–08 show the consequences.','client-runtime'),
       ('O12','Outgoing execute.protocolVersion now comes from the package constant rather than the stored daemon record. Compatible records can yield identical bytes; the changed decision is which authority supplies that field.','unexplained','The field-source change is visible in main’s dispatcher and the final client runtime. No separate rationale was located in the inspected record.','client-runtime'),
      ], sources=['rules','client','client-runtime','routing','manifest','inspector','diagnostic-test','status-test','stop-test','entry-test-main','worker-test','policy-plan-148','vitest','reachability','storage']),
]

